  "response": "## Analyse\n\nLe fichier `tools/summarize.py` doit implémenter un scoring TF (Term Frequency) simplifié pour prioriser les phrases contenant les mots les plus fréquents. L'approche : calculer la fréquence de chaque mot, scorer chaque phrase par la somme des fréquences de ses mots, puis sélectionner les top phrases en ordre d'apparition.\n\n```python path: tools/summarize.py\nimport re\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef summarize(text, num_sentences=3):\n    \"\"\"\n    Summarize text using simplified TF scoring.\n    \n    Args:\n        text: Input text to summarize\n        num_sentences: Number of sentences to include in summary\n    \n    Returns:\n        Summary string with top-scored sentences in original order\n    \"\"\"\n    if not text or not text.strip():\n        return \"\"\n    \n    # Split into sentences (simple approach: period, exclamation, question)\n    sentences = re.split(r'[.!?]+', text)\n    sentences = [s.strip() for s in sentences if s.strip()]\n    \n    if len(sentences) <= num_sentences:\n        return '. '.join(sentences) + '.'\n    \n    # Tokenize and calculate word frequencies (TF)\n    words = re.findall(r'\\b\\w+\\b', text.lower())\n    word_freq = Counter(words)\n    \n    # Filter out very common stop words (minimal set)\n    stop_words = {\n        'the', 'a', 'an', 'and', 'or', 'but', 'in', 'on', 'at', 'to', 'for',\n        'of', 'with', 'by', 'from', 'is', 'are', 'was', 'were', 'be', 'been',\n        'have', 'has', 'had', 'do', 'does', 'did', 'will', 'would', 'could',\n        'should', 'may', 'might', 'must', 'can', 'this', 'that', 'these', 'those',\n        'i', 'you', 'he', 'she', 'it', 'we', 'they', 'what', 'which', 'who',\n        'when', 'where', 'why', 'how'\n    }\n    \n    for word in stop_words:\n        word_freq.pop(word, None)\n    \n    # Score each sentence by sum of word frequencies\n    sentence_scores = []\n    for idx, sentence in enumerate(sentences):\n        words_in_sentence = re.findall(r'\\b\\w+\\b', sentence.lower())\n        score = sum(word_freq.get(word, 0) for word in words_in_sentence)\n        sentence_scores.append((idx, score, sentence))\n    \n    # Select top sentences by score, then sort by original order\n    top_sentences = sorted(sentence_scores, key=lambda x: x[1], reverse=True)[:num_sentences]\n    top_sentences = sorted(top_sentences, key=lambda x: x[0])\n    \n    # Reconstruct summary\n    summary = '. '.join([s[2] for s in top_sentences]) + '.'\n    return summary\n```\n\n```bash\npython -c \"\nfrom tools.summarize import summarize\n\n# Test 1: Basic functionality\ntext = 'Python is great. Python is powerful. Java is also good. Python is fast.'\nresult = summarize(text, num_sentences=2)\nprint('Test 1 - Basic:', repr(result))\nassert len(result) > 0, 'Summary should not be empty'\nassert 'Python' in result, 'Top word should be in summary'\n\n# Test 2: Empty input\nresult = summarize('', num_sentences=2)\nprint('Test 2 - Empty:', repr(result))\nassert result == '', 'Empty input should return empty string'\n\n# Test 3: Fewer sentences than requested\ntext = 'First sentence. Second sentence.'\nresult = summarize(text, num_sentences=5)\nprint('Test 3 - Few sentences:', repr(result))\nassert 'First' in result and 'Second' in result, 'All sentences should be included'\n\nprint('✓ All validation tests passed')\n\"\n```",
